{
  "step_1": "The decision reason compares the maximum and minimum outcomes between lotteries to determine which lottery has more favorable comparisons. Lottery A offers a certain outcome of 1500 Euros, so its maximum and minimum are both 1500. Lottery B offers either 3000 Euros (with 50% probability) or -3000 Euros (with 50% probability), so its maximum is 3000 and its minimum is -3000. A favorable comparison would be when a lottery has a higher outcome than the other in either the maximum or minimum. Comparing maxima: 3000 > 1500 → B has a higher maximum. Comparing minima: -3000 < 1500 → B has a lower minimum. Thus, B has one favorable comparison (higher maximum) and one unfavorable (lower minimum). The reason prefers the lottery with more favorable comparisons. Since B has one favorable and one unfavorable, it has one favorable comparison. A has zero favorable comparisons (since its min and max are both 1500, which is less than 3000 and greater than -3000). Therefore, according to the reason, B has one favorable comparison, A has zero, so B should be preferred. The reason is applicable to the decision problem and can be evaluated using the outcomes and probabilities.",
